The [#4471 page](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue and one fix queued. That is wrong. The reports
merged into #4471 hold three problems with three different causes. The queued fix (the parser) cannot
fix two of them.

<Callout>
Not "known, one fix". The parser fix covers most reports, not all. Two problems have no ticket of their own in the ticket system. They are filed as issues 2 and 3 in this repo.
</Callout>

## The three problems

1. **Parser reads dates month first** ([issue 1](https://github.com/ReaziS/megadev-training/issues/1)). A day-first tenant gets day and month flipped. This is the cause #4471 is named for. The fix is queued. It is the largest group in the table below (`parser`).
2. **The booking-v3 form hints MM/DD/YYYY** ([issue 2](https://github.com/ReaziS/megadev-training/issues/2)). Tenants that write the day first type what the hint tells them, month first, and the form reads it their way. The parser is not involved, so the parser fix does not help. This is the `hint` row.
3. **A date lands one day off** ([issue 3](https://github.com/ReaziS/megadev-training/issues/3)). No month/day swap. The wording (two cities, an invite before the booking) points at time zones. Confidence is medium; engineering has to confirm it. The desk already flagged these in its notes, and the table counts them under `parser` because of their tenant.

## What was merged

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The `parser` row includes the one-day-move reports, so its real parser count is the row minus the group in problem 3:

<OneDayMove ticket={4471} />

## How to tell them apart

Do not use the intake tag. It is a guess from the reporter's browser and is wrong for dozens of reports. Look up the tenant: its locale tells you whether it writes the day first, and its booking form tells you which hint its people saw. Then check the channel: `form` at a booking-v3 tenant is `hint`. A date one day off with no swap is a third kind.

## Lessons

- A big ticket is not always one bug reported many times. Merging on the subject line hid two problems under one.
- Count once per problem, not once per ticket.

